WEBVTT

1
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.417
<i>There were once passageways
to the old world,</i>

2
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:12.250
<i>strange trails, hidden paths.</i>

3
00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:16.750
<i>You'd turn a corner
and suddenly find yourself face-to-face</i>

4
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.125
<i>with the great mystery,
the foundation of all things.</i>

5
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:26.000
<i>And even though
that old world is gone now,</i>

6
00:01:26.792 --> 00:01:30.500
<i>even though it's been rolled up
like a scroll and put somewhere,</i>

7
00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.208
<i>you can still feel the echo of it.</i>

8
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.708
We're clear.

9
00:02:02.792 --> 00:02:04.792
<i>His name was Robert Grainier,</i>

10
00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.792
<i>and he lived more than 80 years</i>

11
00:02:07.875 --> 00:02:10.458
<i>in and around
the town of Bonners Ferry, Idaho.</i>

12
00:02:12.542 --> 00:02:17.625
<i>In his time, he traveled west
within a few dozen miles of the Pacific,</i>

13
00:02:17.708 --> 00:02:19.958
<i>though he'd never seen the ocean itself,</i>

14
00:02:20.042 --> 00:02:24.667
<i>and as far east as the town of Libby,
40 miles inside Montana.</i>

15
00:02:28.375 --> 00:02:31.417
<i>When he was sent by himself
to the town of Fry, Idaho,</i>

16
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.583
<i>he was six, or possibly seven.</i>

17
00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:38.333
<i>He never knew for sure
the year or day of his birth.</i>

18
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.667
<i>How he had lost his original parents,</i>

19
00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:48.625
<i>nobody ever told him.</i>

20
00:02:51.875 --> 00:02:55.917
<i>One of his earliest memories
was that of observing the mass deportation</i>

21
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.792
<i>of 100 or more Chinese families
from the town.</i>

22
00:03:00.292 --> 00:03:03.292
<i>Grainier was baffled
by the casualness of the violence.</i>

23
00:03:05.917 --> 00:03:07.042
Come on up here.

24
00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:08.875
Please, young fella.

25
00:03:09.542 --> 00:03:14.125
I've been cut behind the knee
by this fella they call Big-Ear Al.

26
00:03:15.208 --> 00:03:18.083
And I have to say,
I… I know he's killed me.

27
00:03:18.792 --> 00:03:21.292
<i>There were other memories
that he pushed from his mind</i>

28
00:03:21.375 --> 00:03:23.208
<i>any time they arose.</i>

29
00:03:26.958 --> 00:03:29.458
<i>He quit attending school
in his early teens,</i>

30
00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:33.458
<i>and the next two decades passed
without much direction or purpose.</i>

31
00:03:34.792 --> 00:03:37.333
<i>He felt that nothing much
attracted his interest</i>

32
00:03:38.042 --> 00:03:42.250
<i>until, that is, he met Gladys Olding.</i>

33
00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:52.458
<i>Gladys introduced herself</i>

34
00:03:52.542 --> 00:03:54.667
<i>as if women did things
like that every day.</i>

35
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:55.583
Hello.

36
00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:56.833
<i>And maybe they did.</i>

37
00:03:56.917 --> 00:03:57.917
Hello.

38
00:03:58.625 --> 00:03:59.667
I, uh…

39
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.667
I… I haven't seen you here before.

40
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.042
Oh, no, I've never, um… Uh…

41
00:04:04.833 --> 00:04:07.667
- First time?
- My... My cousin, yeah, brought me.

42
00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.000
Well, his wife is very much...

43
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:11.833
I'm… I'm Robert.

44
00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:13.958
I'm… I'm Gladys.

45
00:04:15.958 --> 00:04:18.083
- Nice to meet you.
- Nice to meet you.

46
00:04:22.917 --> 00:04:25.250
<i>He found he suddenly
had more interest in church</i>

47
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:27.083
<i>than he ever had before.</i>

48
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.500
<i>…fail you
He promised</i>

49
00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:35.333
<i>Believe Him, and all will be well</i>

50
00:04:35.417 --> 00:04:38.458
<i>Not three months later,
they were inseparable.</i>

51
00:04:39.042 --> 00:04:42.833
Right now, I could
just about understand everything there is.

52
00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.333
What are you thinking about?

53
00:04:58.208 --> 00:04:59.625
Well…

54
00:05:04.625 --> 00:05:07.042
I was thinking
that we ought to get married.

55
00:05:10.708 --> 00:05:11.708
What?

56
00:05:15.458 --> 00:05:16.625
We are married.

57
00:05:20.917 --> 00:05:23.833
All we need now is a ceremony to prove it.

58
00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:39.750
And our cabin should have a window
looking out towards the river.

59
00:05:41.208 --> 00:05:42.208
<i>Okay.</i>

60
00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:44.958
<i>- We'll need a dog.
- Mmm.</i>

61
00:05:46.083 --> 00:05:48.417
<i>- I always wanted a dog.
- Mmm.</i>

62
00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:49.750
<i>Okay.</i>

63
00:05:49.833 --> 00:05:51.500
Open the door. Come on.

64
00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:52.583
Okay.

65
00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:58.167
- Hello. Come in.
- What an amazing house you have here.

66
00:05:58.250 --> 00:05:59.292
Can I come in?

67
00:05:59.375 --> 00:06:00.917
Look at this place.

68
00:06:03.125 --> 00:06:06.833
- We'll have the bed right here.
- Facing here. And…

69
00:06:06.917 --> 00:06:08.833
- Window?
- Yeah, here.

70
00:06:10.292 --> 00:06:11.292
Another here.

71
00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:14.000
<i>I want you to say my name one more time.</i>

72
00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:18.000
I just love it when it comes from you.

73
00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:20.208
The sound of it.

74
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Robert.

75
00:06:30.583 --> 00:06:33.792
<i>All of a sudden,
life made sense to Grainier,</i>

76
00:06:34.583 --> 00:06:37.167
<i>as if he'd been pulling hard the wrong way</i>

77
00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:40.125
<i>and was now turned around
and headed downstream.</i>

78
00:06:43.083 --> 00:06:45.625
<i>The couple built a cabin
on an acre of land,</i>

79
00:06:45.708 --> 00:06:47.958
<i>and there, alongside the Moyie River,</i>

80
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.375
<i>began a little life together.</i>

81
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.958
<i>In those years,
Grainier's work took him far from home.</i>

82
00:07:02.042 --> 00:07:05.292
<i>He worked alongside men
who came from faraway lands</i>

83
00:07:05.375 --> 00:07:06.875
<i>he'd never even heard of,</i>

84
00:07:07.375 --> 00:07:10.833
<i>places like Shanghai and Chattanooga.</i>

85
00:07:13.083 --> 00:07:16.958
<i>It was a comfort to him how easily
they all fell in with each other,</i>

86
00:07:17.042 --> 00:07:19.000
<i>became temporary families.</i>

87
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.833
<i>But then, in the summer of 1917,</i>

88
00:07:24.333 --> 00:07:27.208
<i>he worked a job building
the Robinson Gorge Bridge</i>

89
00:07:27.292 --> 00:07:29.625
<i>for the Spokane International Railroad.</i>

90
00:07:30.792 --> 00:07:33.708
<i>He'd never taken up
with a railroad crew before,</i>

91
00:07:34.458 --> 00:07:36.167
<i>and he wished he'd never had.</i>

92
00:07:38.042 --> 00:07:40.042
All right, boys, he's right over here.

93
00:07:41.667 --> 00:07:44.292
- That young boy working on that beam.
- There he is.

94
00:07:45.333 --> 00:07:46.500
Go on. Get him.

95
00:07:47.708 --> 00:07:48.750
Bring him on up here.

96
00:07:48.833 --> 00:07:49.833
Hey, come on.

97
00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:53.750
Bring him on up.

98
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:55.542
What'd he… What'd he do?

99
00:07:56.417 --> 00:07:58.000
Come on up. Come on up.

100
00:08:00.833 --> 00:08:02.250
- Hold on.
- Hey, you.

101
00:08:02.333 --> 00:08:03.750
Yeah, but wha… what's he done?

102
00:08:03.833 --> 00:08:06.333
Hey!

103
00:08:06.417 --> 00:08:07.708
One, two…

104
00:08:09.083 --> 00:08:11.000
Get him up here! That's it.

105
00:08:11.083 --> 00:08:13.125
Bring him up. Take him on out.

106
00:08:17.417 --> 00:08:18.750
What'd he actually do?

107
00:08:18.833 --> 00:08:20.250
I don't even know.

108
00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:22.500
What? Hey.

109
00:09:21.375 --> 00:09:23.083
- Holy…
- Yo, what is he doing?

110
00:09:25.625 --> 00:09:28.792
You boys have shown
this old river valley who's boss.

111
00:09:28.875 --> 00:09:31.667
You helped save Spokane International.

112
00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.458
Eleven miles it used to take
to get around this gorge.

113
00:09:34.542 --> 00:09:37.083
And you opened up
a new part of the country.

114
00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.250
I know it ain't
the Great Pyramids of Egypt,

115
00:09:40.333 --> 00:09:44.042
but I think you boys have done something
pretty darn incredible.

116
00:09:47.583 --> 00:09:50.958
<i>Many years later,
a bridge made of concrete and steel</i>

117
00:09:51.042 --> 00:09:53.167
<i>would be built ten miles upstream,</i>

118
00:09:53.833 --> 00:09:55.500
<i>rendering this one obsolete.</i>

119
00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:06.750
Now,

120
00:10:07.833 --> 00:10:09.208
let's see if she holds.

121
00:10:30.167 --> 00:10:31.167
Whoo!

122
00:12:09.292 --> 00:12:10.958
I missed you.

123
00:12:11.042 --> 00:12:12.208
I missed you.

124
00:12:12.958 --> 00:12:14.375
Hello.

125
00:12:16.292 --> 00:12:17.500
Oh, Gladys.

126
00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.417
- Oh God.
- God, yes.

127
00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.333
Oh, shh. Shh.

128
00:12:26.417 --> 00:12:28.083
I just got her to sleep.

129
00:12:29.417 --> 00:12:30.542
Is she…

130
00:12:31.333 --> 00:12:32.333
Go see her.

131
00:12:35.333 --> 00:12:37.833
- She's just gotten so big.
- Mmm.

132
00:12:39.625 --> 00:12:41.625
She's starting to look like you.

133
00:12:41.708 --> 00:12:42.750
Mmm.

134
00:12:42.833 --> 00:12:45.333
At least she's starting
to favor one of us.

135
00:12:50.667 --> 00:12:52.500
No broken fingers this time?

136
00:12:53.083 --> 00:12:54.458
I don't think so.

137
00:12:55.625 --> 00:12:57.417
I didn't think you'd be home this soon.

138
00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:00.417
I just couldn't stop walking
until I got here.

139
00:13:06.417 --> 00:13:08.000
- Look at that.
- Oh.

140
00:13:11.292 --> 00:13:14.458
I didn't expect that much
after what you already sent.

141
00:13:14.958 --> 00:13:17.167
We hit a soft stand at the end there.

142
00:13:23.583 --> 00:13:24.917
Whoa, whoa.

143
00:13:34.208 --> 00:13:36.208
Let's have a big dinner tonight.

144
00:13:36.292 --> 00:13:38.375
Well, don't go to too much trouble.

145
00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.792
No, come on. Let me show you something.

146
00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.542
You see any of the same old guys?

147
00:14:02.625 --> 00:14:05.208
Yeah. I saw a couple from the Oregon job.

148
00:14:05.292 --> 00:14:07.583
They got good stories?

149
00:14:08.625 --> 00:14:12.417
Yeah. They got good stories,
whether any of them are true or not.

150
00:14:18.417 --> 00:14:20.500
She's watching the candle. Look.

151
00:14:21.875 --> 00:14:23.833
Look at that. Can you see?

152
00:14:25.042 --> 00:14:26.500
Isn't that nice?

153
00:14:27.042 --> 00:14:28.042
Hey, honey.

154
00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:30.333
Yeah.

155
00:14:35.542 --> 00:14:36.542
Mmm.

156
00:14:37.708 --> 00:14:39.625
You want me to feed you?

157
00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:40.708
Hmm?

158
00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:42.667
- Give me some of that chicken.
- Mmm…

159
00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.792
Mmm…

160
00:14:49.708 --> 00:14:51.083
- Oh, come on.
- Mmm.

161
00:14:53.375 --> 00:14:55.375
- She need to sleep?
- Yeah.

162
00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:03.500
Oh.

163
00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:05.500
Yes.

164
00:15:18.042 --> 00:15:19.125
What do you think?

165
00:15:23.375 --> 00:15:25.583
It's a pretty funny-looking bassinet.

166
00:15:28.958 --> 00:15:31.208
It's a fish trap.

167
00:15:32.208 --> 00:15:33.542
Oh.

168
00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:34.875
Bassinet.

169
00:16:29.958 --> 00:16:31.000
Uh…

170
00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:34.958
Did she, uh, wake up?

171
00:16:39.208 --> 00:16:40.292
Was she crying?

172
00:16:41.125 --> 00:16:43.500
Not too bad.

173
00:16:44.208 --> 00:16:46.167
She just woke up hungry.

174
00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:47.250
Mmm.

175
00:16:50.417 --> 00:16:53.083
Do you know what all her cries mean?

176
00:16:54.417 --> 00:16:55.792
Most of them, I guess.

177
00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.917
How much does she know, do you think?

178
00:17:02.417 --> 00:17:03.417
I don't know.

179
00:17:03.958 --> 00:17:04.958
Hmm.

180
00:17:05.833 --> 00:17:08.083
As much as a dog pup?

181
00:17:10.125 --> 00:17:14.417
Well, a dog pup can live on its own
after its mama weans it away.

182
00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.500
A baby couldn't just go off and live
after it was weaned.

183
00:17:21.333 --> 00:17:25.042
A dog knows more than a baby
till the baby knows its words,

184
00:17:25.125 --> 00:17:27.125
and not just a few words.

185
00:17:28.792 --> 00:17:31.375
A dog raised around the house
knows some words too…

186
00:17:31.458 --> 00:17:33.708
…as many as a baby at least.

187
00:17:35.458 --> 00:17:36.875
What words?

188
00:17:38.375 --> 00:17:40.167
I just want to hear your voice.

189
00:17:43.167 --> 00:17:45.958
Well, fetch

190
00:17:46.042 --> 00:17:49.042
and come.

191
00:17:50.792 --> 00:17:53.792
Lay and sit

192
00:17:54.792 --> 00:17:56.208
and roll over.

193
00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:00.958
Whatever it knows to do,
it knows the words.

194
00:18:05.958 --> 00:18:08.417
Do you think that she knows
that I'm her daddy?

195
00:18:10.292 --> 00:18:11.500
Of course she does.

196
00:18:13.792 --> 00:18:15.458
Deep down, she knows it…

197
00:18:17.458 --> 00:18:21.250
Even if she doesn't know she knows it yet.

198
00:18:25.708 --> 00:18:26.708
Don't worry.

199
00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:30.750
You'll have plenty of time
to be her daddy.

200
00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:00.750
Nice having you home.

201
00:19:01.375 --> 00:19:02.375
Hmm.

202
00:19:04.083 --> 00:19:06.708
- I don't want you to disappear.
- Mmm.

203
00:19:11.292 --> 00:19:12.292
There you go.

204
00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:14.500
Yeah.

205
00:19:16.083 --> 00:19:18.250
- No. Don't eat it.
- Does it smell nice?

206
00:19:20.958 --> 00:19:22.875
Sorry. Just one more minute.

207
00:19:28.792 --> 00:19:30.792
Should I put my hand in my pocket?

208
00:19:31.958 --> 00:19:33.167
What do you think?

209
00:19:34.792 --> 00:19:36.917
Oop.

210
00:19:43.125 --> 00:19:46.667
<i>It seemed that as soon as
Grainier felt used to being at home,</i>

211
00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:50.875
<i>logging season would come back around,
and it was time to leave again.</i>

212
00:19:57.083 --> 00:19:59.833
<i>His work was populated with itinerant men,</i>

213
00:20:00.917 --> 00:20:03.583
<i>most without homes, without families.</i>

214
00:20:04.167 --> 00:20:08.875
<i>They moved from job to job,
state to state, as the work dictated.</i>

215
00:20:11.042 --> 00:20:13.708
<i>And though little note
was made of them in this world,</i>

216
00:20:14.333 --> 00:20:16.792
<i>they left a lasting impression
on Grainier.</i>

217
00:20:23.917 --> 00:20:26.375
<i>He once worked alongside a man
two full months</i>

218
00:20:26.458 --> 00:20:28.583
<i>without exchanging a single word.</i>

219
00:20:31.917 --> 00:20:35.333
So I said, "Buddy,
I wasn't looking for that kind of gold."

220
00:20:37.833 --> 00:20:41.167
What about you, mister?
You ever been down to California?

221
00:20:44.167 --> 00:20:47.417
Ain't there any place in this world
a man can get some peace?

222
00:20:50.167 --> 00:20:53.542
<i>Those were the only words
Grainier ever heard the man say.</i>

223
00:20:54.083 --> 00:20:56.000
<i>They remained with him always.</i>

224
00:20:57.833 --> 00:21:01.958
<i>Another was Hank Healy,
who made his home in the trunk of a tree.</i>

225
00:21:02.583 --> 00:21:05.083
And it comforts me,
you get what I'm saying?

226
00:21:05.167 --> 00:21:06.208
I got a problem…

227
00:21:06.292 --> 00:21:07.750
<i>There was Apostle Frank,</i>

228
00:21:08.417 --> 00:21:11.667
<i>a faller who spoke about the Bible
with such familiarity</i>

229
00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:15.000
<i>as to suggest he'd been there
when it was all written down.</i>

230
00:21:15.083 --> 00:21:17.458
It's like that old boy Balaam
in the Bible.

231
00:21:18.042 --> 00:21:22.125
Sometimes God has to find strange ways
to tell you what you need to hear.

232
00:21:22.208 --> 00:21:26.333
Sometimes it's a donkey talking to you,
sometimes it's a who knows.

233
00:21:26.417 --> 00:21:27.833
I was in Nebraska once.

234
00:21:27.917 --> 00:21:30.500
The Lord spoke to my heart,
told me to go to Omaha.

235
00:21:30.583 --> 00:21:33.708
When I got off the train,
the sign said "Opelika."

236
00:21:33.792 --> 00:21:35.375
I said, "Opelika?"

237
00:21:35.458 --> 00:21:38.042
And that's when I knew
my eyes were busted.

238
00:21:38.125 --> 00:21:41.625
Praise God and hallelujah.

239
00:21:41.708 --> 00:21:43.333
I think that's it.

240
00:21:43.417 --> 00:21:45.917
- You get what I'm saying in general?
- Mm-hmm.

241
00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:47.625
So he touched his hip, God did.

242
00:21:47.708 --> 00:21:50.625
And for the rest of Jacob's life,
he walked with a bad limp.

243
00:21:50.708 --> 00:21:52.250
See, Jacob was an all-rounder.

244
00:21:52.333 --> 00:21:55.208
Handled his affairs
with no help from nobody.

245
00:21:55.292 --> 00:21:57.458
But in the end, that's not what the…

246
00:21:57.958 --> 00:21:58.958
Who's that?

247
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.500
Excuse me, gentlemen.
I'm sorry to interrupt you.

248
00:22:03.125 --> 00:22:06.292
Is one among you called Sam Loving
from New Mexico?

249
00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:11.292
Alternatively known as, um…

250
00:22:11.375 --> 00:22:12.500
…Buckskin Sam

251
00:22:13.208 --> 00:22:15.417
in southern Arizona
and parts of California.

252
00:22:16.542 --> 00:22:19.000
I've been trying to find this man
for a very long time

253
00:22:19.083 --> 00:22:21.417
to deliver a message to him.

254
00:22:30.125 --> 00:22:31.375
- Oh.
- Oh, shoot.

255
00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:44.000
Jeez! Come on.

256
00:22:44.083 --> 00:22:45.125
Goodness.

257
00:22:45.208 --> 00:22:46.208
The hell?

258
00:22:51.875 --> 00:22:53.375
That man shot my brother,

259
00:22:54.417 --> 00:22:57.292
Martin Brown, in cold blood
in Gallup, New Mexico,

260
00:22:57.792 --> 00:22:59.167
August 5th, '93.

261
00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.125
He killed him
only because of the color of his skin.

262
00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:07.458
If any of you take offense
to what I've just done here,

263
00:23:07.958 --> 00:23:09.958
let's have it out
before I leave this place.

264
00:23:10.625 --> 00:23:13.792
I do not intend to spend the rest
of my days looking over my shoulder.

265
00:23:19.958 --> 00:23:21.000
Very good, then.

266
00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:25.875
I'm sorry to have interrupted your work.

267
00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:32.042
Big trees.
Never knew trees could get this big.

268
00:23:37.208 --> 00:23:40.333
<i>At the heart of the cut
was a man named Arn Peeples,</i>

269
00:23:40.833 --> 00:23:42.917
<i>a gadabout of unknown origin</i>

270
00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:46.375
<i>whose real use
was occasional but specific.</i>

271
00:24:10.042 --> 00:24:12.542
You Minnesota boys
might want to close your ears.

272
00:24:27.917 --> 00:24:31.250
- Uh, kaboom.
- Told y'all it wouldn't work.

273
00:24:43.667 --> 00:24:45.917
- Don't touch that.
- Yeah. We won't.

274
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.125
- Don't even look at it crossways.
- Not even looking.

275
00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:56.875
<i>He was the oldest man on most jobs,</i>

276
00:24:56.958 --> 00:25:00.375
<i>always yammering,
staying out of the way of hard work.</i>

277
00:25:00.458 --> 00:25:03.250
You let me at that hatchet
if you boys need a break.

278
00:25:04.042 --> 00:25:06.042
I get to chopping,
you come to work in the morning,

279
00:25:06.083 --> 00:25:08.208
the chips won't yet be settled
from yesterday.

280
00:25:09.333 --> 00:25:11.292
I'm made for this summer logging.

281
00:25:12.292 --> 00:25:15.250
I don't get my gears turning smooth
till it's over 100.

282
00:25:16.667 --> 00:25:17.667
That's good.

283
00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:23.500
All this over a little rain.

284
00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:27.042
In my day,
we worked around the whole clock,

285
00:25:27.125 --> 00:25:28.792
not just when it suited you.

286
00:25:31.625 --> 00:25:35.125
Back then,
we'd riddle a bole with auger holes.

287
00:25:35.208 --> 00:25:39.292
Sometimes we had to wait a week
for a good wind to topple them behemoths.

288
00:25:39.792 --> 00:25:42.417
And then they all came
tumbling down at once.

289
00:25:44.292 --> 00:25:47.333
Trees twice as big
as any you're cutting around here.

290
00:25:47.417 --> 00:25:49.708
I worked on a peak
outside of Bisbee, Arizona,

291
00:25:49.792 --> 00:25:52.375
where we was only 11, 12 miles
from the sun.

292
00:25:53.333 --> 00:25:55.875
A hundred and sixteen degrees
on the thermometer.

293
00:25:56.375 --> 00:25:58.375
And every degree was a foot long.

294
00:26:00.542 --> 00:26:02.083
And that was in the shade.

295
00:26:03.375 --> 00:26:05.000
And there weren't no shade.

296
00:26:06.625 --> 00:26:08.708
He's coming up. Here he comes.

297
00:26:21.875 --> 00:26:23.875
Oh!

298
00:26:27.042 --> 00:26:29.417
Whoo!

299
00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:32.583
<i>If the Lord was a redwood</i>

300
00:26:33.958 --> 00:26:36.625
<i>Would you try to cut Him down?</i>

301
00:26:38.083 --> 00:26:43.417
<i>Or climb up His loving branches
And look around?</i>

302
00:26:45.125 --> 00:26:49.708
<i>If the river was the tears
Of those who have passed…</i>

303
00:26:49.792 --> 00:26:52.083
Quit your singing!
I'm trying to sleep over here.

304
00:26:52.167 --> 00:26:54.583
- Arn, go to bed.
- Stop singing!

305
00:26:54.667 --> 00:26:55.667
Hey, Arn.

306
00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:58.042
I won't… I won't sing no more.

307
00:26:58.125 --> 00:26:59.167
No, no, no.

308
00:27:01.125 --> 00:27:05.083
You told Adrien
that these tents are from the Civil War?

309
00:27:05.583 --> 00:27:08.542
That's right. Union infantry.

310
00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:13.708
After that, they went to the US Cavalry
for the Indian campaigns.

311
00:27:14.917 --> 00:27:18.833
These old things have served longer
than the people they sheltered.

312
00:27:19.375 --> 00:27:22.750
Rough canvas, but they'll probably be here
long after we're gone.

313
00:27:23.375 --> 00:27:24.375
Hmm.

314
00:27:26.458 --> 00:27:28.458
What's keeping you awake over there?

315
00:27:29.792 --> 00:27:30.917
Oh, uh…

316
00:27:33.583 --> 00:27:35.958
Arn, do you… do you think that…

317
00:27:37.917 --> 00:27:40.875
The bad things
that we do follow us through life?

318
00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:42.500
I don't know.

319
00:27:44.708 --> 00:27:47.875
I've seen bad men raised up
and good men brought to their knees.

320
00:27:49.958 --> 00:27:51.500
I reckon if I could figure it out,

321
00:27:51.583 --> 00:27:54.292
I'd be sleeping next to someone
a lot better-looking than you.

322
00:28:02.292 --> 00:28:04.375
What was that song that you were singing?

323
00:28:04.958 --> 00:28:08.042
Uh, it… it don't have a name.
It just come to me.

324
00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:12.208
Why don't you sing a little more?

325
00:28:12.708 --> 00:28:16.208
No, no. I… I don't want to upset
the Minnesota boys.

326
00:28:17.667 --> 00:28:20.208
Well, wait a minute. I…

327
00:28:23.667 --> 00:28:24.667
Here.

328
00:28:45.208 --> 00:28:48.875
<i>The crews began to move
deeper into the forest with each job.</i>

329
00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:54.000
<i>There seemed to be no end
to the world's appetite for lumber.</i>

330
00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:57.708
<i>So they worked
from sunrise until suppertime,</i>

331
00:28:57.792 --> 00:29:02.708
<i>felling spruce, cedar, and tamarack,
Doug fir, and white pine,</i>

332
00:29:02.792 --> 00:29:06.333
<i>utterly changing the face
of the mountainside in the process.</i>

333
00:29:07.875 --> 00:29:12.583
<i>And while a good sawyer might correctly
judge 99 times how a fall would go,</i>

334
00:29:14.042 --> 00:29:15.917
<i>the hundredth time might take its toll.</i>

335
00:29:18.083 --> 00:29:20.083
Tree! Hey!

336
00:29:20.167 --> 00:29:22.167
- Help! Help!
- Look out!

337
00:29:22.250 --> 00:29:23.750
Help!

338
00:29:23.833 --> 00:29:25.333
- Get help!
- Help!

339
00:29:25.417 --> 00:29:26.583
Help!

340
00:29:33.958 --> 00:29:34.958
There.

341
00:29:35.833 --> 00:29:39.833
Now they won't just pass out of this world
without nothing to show they was here.

342
00:29:47.083 --> 00:29:48.958
Well, hell.

343
00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:54.208
I wish I could let us all lay off a day,
but it's the company.

344
00:29:55.667 --> 00:30:00.167
The war don't stop needing spruce
just on account of a bad day for us.

345
00:30:08.625 --> 00:30:12.875
<i>Grainier worried more and more
that something terrible was following him,</i>

346
00:30:13.375 --> 00:30:15.500
<i>that death would find him out here,</i>

347
00:30:16.333 --> 00:30:19.375
<i>far away from the only place
he really wanted to be.</i>

348
00:30:20.542 --> 00:30:21.542
Hey…

349
00:30:27.208 --> 00:30:28.375
What do you got?

350
00:30:28.458 --> 00:30:29.667
I got a pine cone.

351
00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:31.917
You got a pine cone?

352
00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:33.625
Oh!

353
00:30:35.333 --> 00:30:36.750
I got a pine cone.

354
00:30:37.875 --> 00:30:39.583
Should we go get the chicken?

355
00:30:42.292 --> 00:30:44.625
Oh, look, there's one.

356
00:30:45.125 --> 00:30:46.750
Chicken right there.

357
00:30:47.458 --> 00:30:49.000
Come here, chicken.

358
00:30:49.083 --> 00:30:50.333
Come here, chicken.

359
00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.083
- They're going inside.
- They're going inside.

360
00:30:53.167 --> 00:30:55.500
- They're going inside!
- Chicken!

361
00:30:56.083 --> 00:30:57.833
- Chicken!
- Chicken!

362
00:30:57.917 --> 00:30:58.917
Chicken!

363
00:30:59.667 --> 00:31:03.458
They're gonna sleep in your bed,
and they're gonna eat your food.

364
00:31:09.667 --> 00:31:12.417
<i>She's like a different person
every time I see her.</i>

365
00:31:15.917 --> 00:31:17.667
<i>Feel like I'm missing her whole life.</i>

366
00:31:19.875 --> 00:31:21.250
<i>I feel it too.</i>

367
00:31:23.375 --> 00:31:25.458
<i>It's all going by so fast.</i>

368
00:31:46.292 --> 00:31:48.375
- You want to shoot?
- No.

369
00:31:48.875 --> 00:31:50.917
Only the deer want me to shoot.

370
00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.167
Gladys.

371
00:31:54.167 --> 00:31:55.250
Don't shoot.

372
00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:57.333
Don't shoot me.

373
00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:00.542
Keep making noise, and I will.

374
00:32:01.167 --> 00:32:02.417
There he is.

375
00:32:17.417 --> 00:32:18.417
Good shot.

376
00:32:26.542 --> 00:32:28.250
What if we came with you?

377
00:32:28.792 --> 00:32:29.792
Hmm?

378
00:32:30.458 --> 00:32:31.667
Out to the cut.

379
00:32:33.958 --> 00:32:35.625
- To the cut?
- Mmm.

380
00:32:35.708 --> 00:32:36.625
What…

381
00:32:36.708 --> 00:32:38.542
Well, I… I'd be helpful.

382
00:32:38.625 --> 00:32:41.375
I… I could make some money
washing clothes.

383
00:32:42.458 --> 00:32:45.083
Now she's not so much to keep up with.

384
00:32:45.792 --> 00:32:47.375
Well, I know you'd be helpful.

385
00:32:47.458 --> 00:32:50.833
You told me some of the others
have had their wives out there.

386
00:32:50.917 --> 00:32:54.125
No, not <i>some </i>of the others,
just that one old man.

387
00:32:54.208 --> 00:32:56.083
What, and that young couple
from California?

388
00:32:56.167 --> 00:32:59.208
Dick Clinton disappeared after a week.

389
00:32:59.292 --> 00:33:03.125
And then, besides,
they didn't even have a little one.

390
00:33:03.208 --> 00:33:04.417
It's, uh…

391
00:33:06.750 --> 00:33:08.292
It's really dangerous.

392
00:33:13.292 --> 00:33:15.375
I'm just trying to find a way, is all.

393
00:33:22.583 --> 00:33:23.583
I know.

394
00:33:25.375 --> 00:33:27.042
I know. I know. I know.

395
00:33:34.125 --> 00:33:36.917
<i>Grainier tried to find work
closer to home,</i>

396
00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:39.083
<i>picking up odd jobs where he could.</i>

397
00:33:40.542 --> 00:33:43.958
<i>But the war was over,
and good-paying work was hard to come by.</i>

398
00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.750
- Sorry to keep you waiting, Robert.
- Yeah, that's okay.

399
00:33:53.833 --> 00:33:55.417
All right.

400
00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:58.000
There you go.

401
00:33:58.042 --> 00:34:00.125
All right. Appreciate it.

402
00:34:00.208 --> 00:34:01.500
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

403
00:34:01.583 --> 00:34:04.458
Uh, if you need anything,
you know where to find me.

404
00:34:04.542 --> 00:34:05.708
Mmm.

405
00:34:06.958 --> 00:34:09.917
<i>Money was tighter for them
than it ever had been.</i>

406
00:34:10.917 --> 00:34:12.833
<i>And though he didn't know it then,</i>

407
00:34:12.917 --> 00:34:17.125
<i>he would always look back
on this time in his life as his happiest.</i>

408
00:34:17.208 --> 00:34:18.542
What do you got there?

409
00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:20.250
Eggs.

410
00:34:23.917 --> 00:34:24.917
Eggs.

411
00:34:26.292 --> 00:34:28.375
Yeah, that is kind of an egg.

412
00:34:29.333 --> 00:34:30.750
There you go.

413
00:34:31.708 --> 00:34:32.792
There you go.

414
00:34:40.417 --> 00:34:43.375
- Tired, Katie? You ready for bed?
- Yeah.

415
00:34:46.125 --> 00:34:47.708
You're getting so big.

416
00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:50.208
You're gonna be carrying me soon.

417
00:35:13.125 --> 00:35:14.958
<i>Maybe there's a better way.</i>

418
00:35:16.833 --> 00:35:19.125
Maybe we turn our acre into a farm.

419
00:35:19.917 --> 00:35:22.542
I could grow double what I do now.

420
00:35:22.625 --> 00:35:27.667
And if we did that, and we saved up enough
to start a little sawmill,

421
00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:29.750
maybe you could be home more.

422
00:35:29.833 --> 00:35:33.750
Get a horse or a mule on credit, I guess.

423
00:35:33.833 --> 00:35:35.375
Yeah, on credit for sure.

424
00:35:36.458 --> 00:35:39.250
The sawmill would take
a bit more doing, you know.

425
00:35:39.333 --> 00:35:41.292
It's not so cheap, but…

426
00:35:41.375 --> 00:35:43.375
Yeah. Well, I didn't say it wouldn't be.

427
00:35:43.458 --> 00:35:46.292
No, but it's a good idea.

428
00:35:47.667 --> 00:35:49.542
It is a good idea.

429
00:35:49.625 --> 00:35:50.625
Yeah.

430
00:36:00.833 --> 00:36:03.333
Come on, Katie. Will you say bye-bye?

431
00:36:03.417 --> 00:36:04.875
Katie, watch this. Ready?

432
00:36:15.708 --> 00:36:16.542
I'll just go.

433
00:36:16.625 --> 00:36:18.000
- Okay.
- Love you.

434
00:36:19.250 --> 00:36:20.375
I'm gonna miss you.

435
00:36:21.917 --> 00:36:24.208
I love you too. All right.

436
00:36:33.083 --> 00:36:35.625
Come on, Kate. Say goodbye to Daddy.

437
00:36:35.708 --> 00:36:37.125
Kate. Come on.

438
00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:41.458
Katie, can you put that down?

439
00:36:52.083 --> 00:36:54.708
Excuse me. Looking for the foreman.

440
00:36:54.792 --> 00:36:56.375
Just keep going that way.

441
00:36:57.333 --> 00:36:58.333
Thank you.

442
00:37:00.917 --> 00:37:03.542
<i>He earned four dollars a day on most jobs,</i>

443
00:37:03.625 --> 00:37:06.208
<i>minus expenses
for what the company provided.</i>

444
00:37:10.292 --> 00:37:13.917
<i>He and Gladys figured that after
one more long stint in the woods,</i>

445
00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:17.417
<i>they'd have enough money
to start building a sawmill back home.</i>

446
00:37:47.792 --> 00:37:51.042
Y'all going on to another job,
or you quitting for the season?

447
00:37:51.125 --> 00:37:52.208
Mmm.

448
00:37:55.125 --> 00:37:56.125
I can't decide.

449
00:37:56.208 --> 00:38:00.083
I ain't never happy
when the job ends for some reason.

450
00:38:01.083 --> 00:38:02.917
I just feel itchy inside.

451
00:38:04.083 --> 00:38:08.083
That's 'cause it's rough work, gentlemen,
not just on the body but on the soul.

452
00:38:09.625 --> 00:38:12.500
We just cut down trees
that have been here for 500 years.

453
00:38:13.708 --> 00:38:16.417
It upsets a man's soul
whether you recognize it or not.

454
00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:20.500
I'll have $200
in my pocket tomorrow morning.

455
00:38:22.333 --> 00:38:25.375
Don't bother my soul. Not one damn bit.

456
00:38:25.875 --> 00:38:29.083
That's 'cause you Minnesota fellas
don't know nothing about history.

457
00:38:29.167 --> 00:38:31.375
These trees are really that old?

458
00:38:31.458 --> 00:38:32.917
Why, some's older even.

459
00:38:33.583 --> 00:38:37.042
This world is intricately
stitched together, boys.

460
00:38:38.167 --> 00:38:42.250
Every thread we pull, we know not
how it affects the design of things.

461
00:38:43.375 --> 00:38:47.042
We're but children on this earth,
pulling bolts out of the Ferris wheel,

462
00:38:47.125 --> 00:38:48.583
thinking ourselves to be gods.

463
00:38:48.667 --> 00:38:51.083
That's horseshit.
I've been to Washington too.

464
00:38:52.458 --> 00:38:55.208
Cut all up through Canada
and back down again.

465
00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:59.667
There's enough logs
for us to cut for 1,000 years.

466
00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:02.667
And then when the last one's cut,

467
00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:06.375
well, first one will be growed up
as big as anything around today.

468
00:39:09.167 --> 00:39:12.042
I remember thinking the same thing
when I was a young man…

469
00:39:13.458 --> 00:39:14.833
The very same thing.

470
00:39:34.375 --> 00:39:37.542
You moving along, Robert?
Or packing up for home?

471
00:39:39.333 --> 00:39:42.000
I'm packing it up, going home.

472
00:39:44.500 --> 00:39:47.125
I miss my wife and my little one.

473
00:39:48.667 --> 00:39:50.417
What's her name? Your missus?

474
00:39:51.458 --> 00:39:52.500
Gladys.

475
00:39:52.583 --> 00:39:53.583
Mmm.

476
00:39:54.083 --> 00:39:57.000
Welsh. That's a highborn name.

477
00:39:58.750 --> 00:40:01.042
That makes sense, if you knew her.

478
00:40:02.625 --> 00:40:04.333
Those old names have power.

479
00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:07.375
The ones that have them are blessed.

480
00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:09.250
Hmm.

481
00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:13.000
You got family somewhere?

482
00:40:15.458 --> 00:40:16.625
My family is…

483
00:40:17.750 --> 00:40:19.875
Everywhere there's a smiling face.

484
00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:24.125
Never been somewhere
I didn't have some family there.

485
00:40:25.708 --> 00:40:26.750
Except for Kansas.

486
00:40:26.833 --> 00:40:29.500
That state is a collection
of savage lunatics.

487
00:40:34.542 --> 00:40:36.208
It's good having you around.

488
00:40:36.792 --> 00:40:40.375
Not too many folks I cross paths with
more than once in this life.

489
00:40:41.333 --> 00:40:44.083
I see it as a blessing
when they're brought back around.

490
00:40:44.750 --> 00:40:45.750
Ah.

491
00:40:48.208 --> 00:40:50.417
I don't know where the years go, Arn.

492
00:40:51.292 --> 00:40:55.583
Well… if you figure it out, let me know.

493
00:40:55.667 --> 00:40:57.625
I'd like to ask for a few back.

494
00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:03.542
<i>It looked certain Arn Peeples
would exit this world in a puff of smoke</i>

495
00:41:03.625 --> 00:41:04.875
<i>with a monstrous noise.</i>

496
00:41:06.167 --> 00:41:08.167
<i>But he went out quite differently.</i>

497
00:41:15.083 --> 00:41:16.750
- Somebody get some help!
- We need help!

498
00:41:16.833 --> 00:41:18.833
- Who is it?
- We got a man down!

499
00:41:18.917 --> 00:41:20.292
Hey, Arn!

500
00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:22.583
Arn?

501
00:41:22.667 --> 00:41:24.500
- Widow-maker got him.
- Arn?

502
00:41:24.583 --> 00:41:26.333
Hey, Arn? Can you hear me?

503
00:41:26.417 --> 00:41:28.042
I'm all right. I just…

504
00:41:28.125 --> 00:41:30.708
Help me sit up. Help me sit up.

505
00:41:30.792 --> 00:41:34.125
All right. Slowly.

506
00:41:34.208 --> 00:41:37.000
- Can we get a little water?
- I saw my sister and her husband.

507
00:41:37.083 --> 00:41:40.125
They was just here.
You know which way they walked to?

508
00:41:40.208 --> 00:41:41.125
Arn…

509
00:41:41.208 --> 00:41:42.958
<i>He had a number of dizzy spells</i>

510
00:41:43.042 --> 00:41:46.750
<i>and grew dreamy and forgetful
over the next few days,</i>

511
00:41:47.292 --> 00:41:49.958
<i>forgetting even his own name
before it was all done.</i>

512
00:41:52.208 --> 00:41:53.208
You hear that?

513
00:41:57.583 --> 00:41:58.792
Beautiful, ain't it?

514
00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:02.333
Just beautiful.

515
00:42:04.792 --> 00:42:06.083
What is, Arn?

516
00:42:09.125 --> 00:42:10.125
All of it.

517
00:42:13.667 --> 00:42:14.833
Every bit of it.

518
00:42:34.375 --> 00:42:36.833
Arn Peeples was my friend.

519
00:42:38.375 --> 00:42:41.000
He… He said that a… a tree was a friend…

520
00:42:44.208 --> 00:42:45.708
If you let it alone.

521
00:42:47.292 --> 00:42:51.000
But the second the blade bit in,
you had yourself a war,

522
00:42:52.208 --> 00:42:54.000
and the tree was a killer.

523
00:42:56.375 --> 00:42:58.833
But he wasn't messing with that tree.

524
00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:01.833
It was… It was just a snag.

525
00:43:04.542 --> 00:43:05.542
So, I…

526
00:43:06.583 --> 00:43:08.875
I… I don't know what meaning
to take from that.

527
00:43:15.583 --> 00:43:17.167
In Jesus's name…

528
00:43:17.667 --> 00:43:19.292
- Amen.
- Amen.

529
00:43:20.792 --> 00:43:22.792
<i>Though Grainier had seen death often,</i>

530
00:43:23.708 --> 00:43:25.875
<i>he'd never lost someone so close to him.</i>

531
00:43:29.958 --> 00:43:32.000
<i>He began to feel a dread</i>

532
00:43:32.083 --> 00:43:34.625
<i>that the snag had been meant for him,</i>

533
00:43:35.292 --> 00:43:37.708
<i>that some punishment was seeking him.</i>

534
00:43:54.375 --> 00:43:55.958
<i>Robert?</i>

535
00:44:05.375 --> 00:44:06.500
What is that?

536
00:44:06.583 --> 00:44:07.875
Oh dear God.

537
00:44:11.625 --> 00:44:13.583
- Come look at this.
- Where is that?

538
00:44:13.667 --> 00:44:16.625
- Are we heading over there?
- That's not far off.

539
00:44:17.792 --> 00:44:19.083
- Wow.
- Wow.

540
00:44:19.167 --> 00:44:20.167
Hey.

541
00:44:21.208 --> 00:44:22.333
What is that?

542
00:44:37.625 --> 00:44:38.875
Mary!

543
00:44:38.958 --> 00:44:41.125
Mary, have you… have you seen Gladys?

544
00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:42.750
No?

545
00:44:44.875 --> 00:44:45.958
Gladys!

546
00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:17.667
Gladys!

547
00:47:43.250 --> 00:47:46.292
<i>For nearly two weeks,
he searched every town in the region,</i>

548
00:47:46.375 --> 00:47:48.208
<i>looking for Gladys and Kate.</i>

549
00:47:50.292 --> 00:47:51.333
Four-fifty.

550
00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:56.125
<i>Finding them nowhere,</i>

551
00:47:56.208 --> 00:47:59.083
<i>he went back to his acre
to await their return.</i>

552
00:47:59.583 --> 00:48:01.083
Hey… Oh, hold on.

553
00:48:01.833 --> 00:48:02.833
This is for you.

554
00:48:05.250 --> 00:48:06.250
Thank you.

555
00:49:24.875 --> 00:49:25.875
Water!

556
00:49:26.333 --> 00:49:27.333
Water?

557
00:49:32.375 --> 00:49:34.042
You gonna water the plants for Mama?

558
00:49:56.417 --> 00:49:58.208
Don't you think it's too much?

559
00:50:02.583 --> 00:50:04.458
How did… How did they deserve that?

560
00:50:07.333 --> 00:50:08.333
Mmm?

561
00:50:12.208 --> 00:50:13.208
Why?

562
00:50:17.000 --> 00:50:18.083
Why?!

563
00:51:02.167 --> 00:51:03.167
Hello, Robert.

564
00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:05.500
You hungry?

565
00:51:22.042 --> 00:51:24.083
I didn't know what I'd find out here.

566
00:51:26.750 --> 00:51:29.667
You wouldn't believe the stories
they're telling about you in town.

567
00:51:33.250 --> 00:51:35.333
I'll pay you back for all this. I promise.

568
00:51:35.417 --> 00:51:36.625
I didn't ask you to.

569
00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:46.750
Well, I sure do appreciate you coming up

570
00:51:48.125 --> 00:51:49.125
to check on me.

571
00:51:59.083 --> 00:52:00.167
Let's take a walk.

572
00:52:09.917 --> 00:52:10.917
Ooh, ooh, ooh.

573
00:52:25.625 --> 00:52:27.708
<i>This is a good spot for a cabin.</i>

574
00:52:30.250 --> 00:52:31.750
<i>Do you like how I say your name?</i>

575
00:52:41.708 --> 00:52:42.750
<i>Robert.</i>

576
00:52:51.917 --> 00:52:52.917
They're…

577
00:53:00.083 --> 00:53:01.083
Th...

578
00:53:01.792 --> 00:53:02.792
They're…

579
00:53:09.292 --> 00:53:11.167
They're just not coming back.

580
00:53:46.625 --> 00:53:47.750
I'm sorry.

581
00:53:55.125 --> 00:53:57.625
I'm… I'm sorry.

582
00:53:59.958 --> 00:54:01.625
I don't know what came over me.

583
00:54:06.083 --> 00:54:07.083
Skin him.

584
00:54:35.833 --> 00:54:38.417
<i>Grainier raised a lean-to at his home site,</i>

585
00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:40.792
<i>and there he lived
through the rest of the summer,</i>

586
00:54:40.875 --> 00:54:42.417
<i>fishing for speckled trout</i>

587
00:54:42.500 --> 00:54:45.375
<i>and hunting for a rare
and flavorful mushroom</i>

588
00:54:45.458 --> 00:54:47.708
<i>the Canadians called morel,</i>

589
00:54:47.792 --> 00:54:50.417
<i>which sprang up
on ground disturbed by fire.</i>

590
00:54:52.542 --> 00:54:54.583
<i>Though he confessed it to no one,</i>

591
00:54:55.083 --> 00:54:59.875
<i>he held some faint hope
that Gladys and Kate might somehow return.</i>

592
00:54:59.958 --> 00:55:02.292
<i>And he wanted to be ready for them
if they did.</i>

593
00:55:09.250 --> 00:55:10.583
Hey!

594
00:55:11.167 --> 00:55:12.167
Get out of there.

595
00:55:19.208 --> 00:55:20.833
Where'd you all come from?

596
00:55:22.750 --> 00:55:24.000
Who do you belong to?

597
00:55:26.375 --> 00:55:27.375
Hello?

598
00:55:28.500 --> 00:55:31.500
Shouldn't y'all be in town?
You look like town dogs.

599
00:55:33.333 --> 00:55:34.875
Come on.

600
00:55:37.208 --> 00:55:38.917
I hope you all like fish.

601
00:55:45.917 --> 00:55:47.792
All right. Close your eyes. Ready?

602
00:55:52.250 --> 00:55:53.917
All right, you're free. Come on.

603
00:55:55.625 --> 00:55:57.667
Are these puppies really yours?

604
00:55:59.792 --> 00:56:01.792
What are you doing all the way out here?

605
00:56:02.333 --> 00:56:03.333
Huh?

606
00:56:03.750 --> 00:56:05.083
How much do you know?

607
00:56:07.167 --> 00:56:08.292
You roll over?

608
00:56:09.917 --> 00:56:10.917
And sit?

609
00:56:12.417 --> 00:56:13.417
Fetch?

610
00:56:31.250 --> 00:56:32.250
<i>Katie!</i>

611
00:56:40.250 --> 00:56:41.250
<i>See that?</i>

612
00:56:50.542 --> 00:56:52.750
What kind of dog do you think they are?

613
00:56:54.542 --> 00:56:55.542
I don't know.

614
00:56:57.417 --> 00:56:58.958
They appear quite wolfish.

615
00:56:59.042 --> 00:57:00.042
Aren't they?

616
00:57:01.583 --> 00:57:03.083
I mean, maybe, uh…

617
00:57:04.667 --> 00:57:08.583
Maybe Red Dog got with a wolf out there

618
00:57:09.292 --> 00:57:11.333
somewhere on her own, you know?

619
00:57:11.417 --> 00:57:13.208
No, that's impossible.

620
00:57:13.875 --> 00:57:14.875
Why not?

621
00:57:15.958 --> 00:57:18.333
Only a he-wolf ever mates.

622
00:57:18.417 --> 00:57:21.458
And that's the chief of the wolf pack.

623
00:57:21.542 --> 00:57:22.667
Uh-huh.

624
00:57:22.750 --> 00:57:25.042
And the she-wolf
he chooses to bear his litter

625
00:57:25.625 --> 00:57:27.833
is the only one that ever comes into heat.

626
00:57:28.583 --> 00:57:29.667
What… What if

627
00:57:31.125 --> 00:57:32.583
Red Dog was in heat?

628
00:57:33.250 --> 00:57:34.500
He chooses only one.

629
00:57:35.958 --> 00:57:39.625
Well, what if…
what if she encountered the chief wolf

630
00:57:41.083 --> 00:57:42.417
at the exact moment?

631
00:57:43.458 --> 00:57:44.583
You know what I mean?

632
00:57:44.667 --> 00:57:49.875
Would he not take her
just for the newness of the experience?

633
00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:56.250
"Newness of the experience"?

634
00:57:56.333 --> 00:57:57.333
Yeah.

635
00:58:00.667 --> 00:58:01.750
What?

636
00:58:01.833 --> 00:58:04.417
"Newness of the experience."

637
00:58:11.875 --> 00:58:14.458
Can you howl? Huh? Can you howl too?

638
00:58:15.042 --> 00:58:16.750
Huh? Can you howl?

639
00:58:20.750 --> 00:58:21.833
Hey, buddy.

640
00:58:21.917 --> 00:58:23.333
Hey. Hey, little one.

641
00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:25.000
Hey.

642
00:58:39.292 --> 00:58:42.958
<i>With Ignatius Jack's help,
he raised the four walls of the cabin</i>

643
00:58:43.042 --> 00:58:45.792
<i>precisely where the old ones had stood.</i>

644
00:58:45.875 --> 00:58:46.875
Red Dog?

645
00:58:48.500 --> 00:58:51.083
<i>He could not bring himself
to rebuild the bedroom.</i>

646
00:58:57.042 --> 00:59:01.167
<i>In that year,
a great comet appeared in the sky,</i>

647
00:59:01.250 --> 00:59:03.833
<i>which many said signaled the end of days.</i>

648
00:59:05.500 --> 00:59:09.875
<i>But after two weeks,
it faded away as quietly as it had come.</i>

649
00:59:17.292 --> 00:59:20.125
<i>The rebuilt cabin
looked much like the first one.</i>

650
00:59:21.625 --> 00:59:24.958
<i>But the emptiness of the place
at times overwhelmed him.</i>

651
00:59:27.667 --> 00:59:31.208
<i>Years passed,
and he found himself still waiting.</i>

652
00:59:32.792 --> 00:59:35.333
<i>Though at this point,
he couldn't say for what.</i>

653
01:00:21.875 --> 01:00:25.417
Get your asses up
on that stand of white pine!

654
01:00:26.750 --> 01:00:31.000
I want every one of those trees
down this mountain

655
01:00:31.083 --> 01:00:34.083
before I wake up on the Lord's day!

656
01:00:34.167 --> 01:00:40.583
After that, I'm docking each one
of you one dollar a day!

657
01:00:40.667 --> 01:00:41.667
Let's go!

658
01:00:52.000 --> 01:00:53.000
Let's go!

659
01:01:06.250 --> 01:01:09.083
Hey. Watch out, old man.
Let me see that thing.

660
01:01:09.167 --> 01:01:10.667
Go get a crosscut or something.

661
01:01:14.458 --> 01:01:16.750
I just don't know what...

662
01:01:43.542 --> 01:01:45.167
Look out! Look out! Look out!

663
01:02:07.958 --> 01:02:09.208
Look out, boys!

664
01:02:15.458 --> 01:02:17.417
Hey, let me know if you want some water.

665
01:02:19.583 --> 01:02:20.833
You'll be okay.

666
01:02:21.333 --> 01:02:23.250
Need some help up?

667
01:02:34.458 --> 01:02:36.208
You mind if I warm up a minute?

668
01:02:36.292 --> 01:02:38.917
Mmm.

669
01:02:43.625 --> 01:02:45.042
- Billy?
- Yeah.

670
01:02:45.833 --> 01:02:46.917
Is that you?

671
01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:51.833
Yes. Do I know you?

672
01:02:52.667 --> 01:02:56.458
Yeah, we cut together
in Salmo-Priest a few years back.

673
01:02:57.208 --> 01:03:00.125
I'm… I was a friend of Arn Peeples.

674
01:03:00.792 --> 01:03:01.792
I'm Robert.

675
01:03:02.667 --> 01:03:03.750
Oh, yes.

676
01:03:04.542 --> 01:03:06.083
Hi, Robert.

677
01:03:06.625 --> 01:03:07.958
It's good to see you.

678
01:03:08.042 --> 01:03:09.042
You too.

679
01:03:09.708 --> 01:03:12.708
I mean,
I can't believe you're still out here.

680
01:03:13.500 --> 01:03:15.583
I can barely keep up at my age.

681
01:03:16.083 --> 01:03:18.792
Ah, well, I just
look after the steam donkey

682
01:03:18.875 --> 01:03:20.958
and keep it watered and greased.

683
01:03:21.833 --> 01:03:25.083
Not much else I can do, but it's a living.

684
01:03:26.125 --> 01:03:27.417
- Mmm.
- Mmm.

685
01:03:32.333 --> 01:03:37.833
Say, Billy, do you think
it's a little different now out here?

686
01:03:39.708 --> 01:03:42.583
I don't know if it's different
or if it's always been this way.

687
01:03:42.667 --> 01:03:45.667
Maybe I was rougher
and just like these boys.

688
01:03:46.208 --> 01:03:48.292
I, uh, just don't remember it.

689
01:03:49.667 --> 01:03:53.625
Well, uh, that is the age-old question,

690
01:03:54.208 --> 01:03:55.208
ain't it, friend?

691
01:03:55.750 --> 01:03:57.000
That is the question.

692
01:03:58.625 --> 01:03:59.625
Yeah.

693
01:04:01.375 --> 01:04:04.417
Say, how is… how is Arn?

694
01:04:07.542 --> 01:04:08.375
How… How is...

695
01:04:08.458 --> 01:04:10.583
I ain't seen him in, gosh…

696
01:04:12.375 --> 01:04:14.292
At least a year, maybe longer.

697
01:04:20.125 --> 01:04:22.333
Yeah. Uh, no.

698
01:04:23.625 --> 01:04:26.292
Yeah. I… I ain't seen him either.

699
01:04:30.167 --> 01:04:32.375
Ah. Ain't that how it goes?

700
01:04:42.208 --> 01:04:43.208
Let me help you.

701
01:04:43.958 --> 01:04:45.042
Here.

702
01:04:45.125 --> 01:04:46.833
My hands are not working today.

703
01:04:46.917 --> 01:04:48.000
Here.

704
01:04:49.000 --> 01:04:50.083
I got you.

705
01:04:51.542 --> 01:04:53.542
- Thank you.
- Yeah. No problem.

706
01:04:53.625 --> 01:04:56.958
<i>And with that,
Grainier was finished as a logger.</i>

707
01:05:41.875 --> 01:05:43.042
<i>The last few years,</i>

708
01:05:43.125 --> 01:05:47.417
<i>he expected some great revelation
about his life would descend upon him.</i>

709
01:05:53.500 --> 01:05:56.458
What's that?

710
01:06:03.333 --> 01:06:04.500
Can you do that?

711
01:06:04.583 --> 01:06:07.083
<i>But as of yet, none had.</i>

712
01:06:07.875 --> 01:06:10.833
<i>And he was beginning to doubt
that one ever would.</i>

713
01:06:15.417 --> 01:06:17.042
Hey.

714
01:06:23.625 --> 01:06:26.542
Not been doing the weeding, huh?

715
01:06:32.792 --> 01:06:35.417
<i>These days,
there was plenty of work in town</i>

716
01:06:35.500 --> 01:06:38.125
<i>for anybody willing
to get around after it.</i>

717
01:06:38.750 --> 01:06:42.208
<i>He came into possession
of a pair of horses and a wagon,</i>

718
01:06:42.292 --> 01:06:44.542
<i>by a sad circumstance, however.</i>

719
01:06:48.958 --> 01:06:49.833
Avery?

720
01:06:49.917 --> 01:06:53.875
<i>Avery Pinkham's heart condition
would have been easily diagnosed and cured</i>

721
01:06:53.958 --> 01:06:56.292
<i>had he been born a generation later.</i>

722
01:06:57.292 --> 01:06:58.542
Mr. Pinkham!

723
01:07:01.042 --> 01:07:03.000
<i>Grainier contracted with the Pinkhams</i>

724
01:07:03.083 --> 01:07:07.000
<i>to buy the horses and wagon
for $300 in installments.</i>

725
01:07:07.083 --> 01:07:10.542
<i>And he kept himself busy
as a freighter of sorts.</i>

726
01:07:12.500 --> 01:07:15.667
<i>The hauling itself was a ticket
to a kind of show,</i>

727
01:07:15.750 --> 01:07:18.708
<i>composed of the follies
and endeavors of his neighbors.</i>

728
01:07:21.667 --> 01:07:23.375
Y'all comfortable back there?

729
01:07:23.458 --> 01:07:24.583
Yes.

730
01:07:24.667 --> 01:07:26.792
<i>The work put him
in closer contact with them</i>

731
01:07:26.875 --> 01:07:28.375
<i>than he had been in years.</i>

732
01:07:29.042 --> 01:07:31.500
<i>And yet, he only felt more alone.</i>

733
01:07:31.583 --> 01:07:32.583
No?

734
01:07:46.792 --> 01:07:49.000
<i>Be gentle with the rabbits.</i>

735
01:07:49.083 --> 01:07:51.500
<i>They don't know
you're not gonna hurt them.</i>

736
01:07:57.917 --> 01:07:59.000
<i>Katie.</i>

737
01:08:22.625 --> 01:08:23.625
Miss, uh…

738
01:08:24.833 --> 01:08:25.833
Miss Thompson?

739
01:08:26.375 --> 01:08:28.708
Claire. You must be Robert.

740
01:08:28.792 --> 01:08:30.458
Uh, yes, ma'am.

741
01:08:30.542 --> 01:08:31.542
Nice to meet you.

742
01:08:31.625 --> 01:08:33.625
Yeah. You too.

743
01:08:33.708 --> 01:08:36.875
Nice to meet you. I was looking for you
at the train station.

744
01:08:36.958 --> 01:08:40.167
Oh yeah, I waited a few minutes for you
before I decided to start walking.

745
01:08:40.250 --> 01:08:41.333
Yeah, I'm really sorry.

746
01:08:41.417 --> 01:08:43.976
It took me a little while
to get these ladies going this morning.

747
01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:45.250
And, uh…

748
01:08:45.333 --> 01:08:47.917
…would you still like me
to take you up to your lookout?

749
01:08:48.000 --> 01:08:51.417
- Oh, if it's not too much trouble.
- No, of course. Jump up.

750
01:08:52.042 --> 01:08:53.292
- You wanna…?
- Yep.

751
01:08:54.792 --> 01:08:55.792
Oh, okay.

752
01:08:57.250 --> 01:08:58.250
All right.

753
01:08:59.125 --> 01:09:00.667
All right. Ready?

754
01:09:02.125 --> 01:09:03.250
All right.

755
01:09:09.583 --> 01:09:11.083
What parts are you from?

756
01:09:11.708 --> 01:09:13.042
Oh, I've been all over.

757
01:09:13.667 --> 01:09:17.292
But I lived a long time
over in Montana. Noxon.

758
01:09:18.333 --> 01:09:19.833
I've never seen Montana.

759
01:09:19.917 --> 01:09:22.333
Oh, beautiful country.
Yeah, worth the trip.

760
01:09:31.708 --> 01:09:33.667
They told me about you, you know?

761
01:09:35.417 --> 01:09:36.500
Who's they?

762
01:09:37.458 --> 01:09:39.167
People who recommended you.

763
01:09:40.708 --> 01:09:41.750
Mmm.

764
01:09:42.333 --> 01:09:43.583
And what did they say?

765
01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:47.333
That you were different.

766
01:09:49.500 --> 01:09:51.083
Ain't everybody different?

767
01:09:53.792 --> 01:09:54.792
No.

768
01:09:55.792 --> 01:09:58.208
Mmm. Hmm.

769
01:10:00.417 --> 01:10:02.333
It's a good thing to be different.

770
01:10:03.917 --> 01:10:05.625
As far as I see it, anyway.

771
01:10:09.917 --> 01:10:13.250
I'm so happy to be here.
This valley is special.

772
01:10:17.042 --> 01:10:21.917
Used to all be under a glacier, you know?
Three thousand feet of ice.

773
01:10:22.000 --> 01:10:25.708
When it broke,
it just flooded the whole region.

774
01:10:27.542 --> 01:10:29.583
Carved out all these valleys.

775
01:10:30.333 --> 01:10:32.083
That's where all those lakes come from.

776
01:10:32.750 --> 01:10:34.833
Can you imagine
if you were back here then?

777
01:10:34.917 --> 01:10:37.792
This big block of ice,
thousands of feet tall,

778
01:10:37.875 --> 01:10:41.708
and just the cracks
and the freezing-cold water.

779
01:10:42.958 --> 01:10:45.625
It must have felt
like the world was coming to an end.

780
01:10:47.792 --> 01:10:48.792
Hmm.

781
01:10:50.042 --> 01:10:52.417
That's where all those myths come from,
you know?

782
01:10:52.500 --> 01:10:53.958
All those flood stories.

783
01:10:54.583 --> 01:10:56.625
All those different religions
all over the world.

784
01:10:56.708 --> 01:10:59.542
It's just the same story,
different slants.

785
01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:03.083
Hey, I didn't mean to be disrespectful

786
01:11:03.167 --> 01:11:04.967
to anything you believe
or anything like that.

787
01:11:05.000 --> 01:11:05.875
I just, you know…

788
01:11:05.958 --> 01:11:07.292
No, no, no.

789
01:11:07.375 --> 01:11:09.208
I just find it fascinating.

790
01:11:09.292 --> 01:11:12.000
Can't help it
when I'm in a place like this. It's just…

791
01:11:16.083 --> 01:11:17.958
The world's an old place.

792
01:11:18.042 --> 01:11:19.042
Yeah.

793
01:11:20.125 --> 01:11:22.167
Probably nothing it hasn't seen by now.

794
01:11:28.750 --> 01:11:32.083
<i>Claire had worked in Europe
as a nurse in the Great War.</i>

795
01:11:32.583 --> 01:11:36.042
<i>Now she was employed
by the newly created US Forest Service</i>

796
01:11:36.125 --> 01:11:40.542
<i>as part of an effort to manage timber cuts
and prevent forest fires.</i>

797
01:11:41.167 --> 01:11:43.583
Oh, uh, thank you.

798
01:11:45.500 --> 01:11:47.125
It was nice to meet you, Robert.

799
01:11:48.875 --> 01:11:51.792
- Thank you for the ride.
- It was very nice to meet you too.

800
01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:54.500
Yeah. Okay.

801
01:11:55.750 --> 01:11:56.792
Uh…

802
01:11:56.875 --> 01:11:57.875
Oh.

803
01:11:58.417 --> 01:12:00.167
- There you go.
- Thank you.

804
01:12:00.250 --> 01:12:01.833
You don't need a hand?

805
01:12:01.917 --> 01:12:03.458
- Nope.
- No? Okay.

806
01:12:06.292 --> 01:12:07.417
Take care now.

807
01:12:37.708 --> 01:12:38.875
Did I scare you?

808
01:12:41.042 --> 01:12:43.042
Oh boy.

809
01:13:54.833 --> 01:13:56.500
<i>Robert.</i>

810
01:14:58.375 --> 01:14:59.375
Katie!

811
01:15:12.292 --> 01:15:14.250
It's all right.

812
01:15:15.458 --> 01:15:17.417
Just gonna put a blanket over…

813
01:15:30.208 --> 01:15:31.667
Mama…

814
01:17:35.250 --> 01:17:36.375
Miss Thompson?

815
01:17:37.958 --> 01:17:39.208
Are you up there?

816
01:17:46.125 --> 01:17:47.500
Robert?

817
01:17:47.583 --> 01:17:49.042
Oh.

818
01:17:49.125 --> 01:17:51.500
What a surprise.

819
01:17:51.583 --> 01:17:52.792
How are you?

820
01:17:52.875 --> 01:17:53.875
Good.

821
01:17:54.542 --> 01:17:57.500
Yeah. I hope I didn't startle you.

822
01:17:57.583 --> 01:17:58.583
No. Not at all.

823
01:17:58.625 --> 01:18:01.583
I was just heading out
for an afternoon survey,

824
01:18:01.667 --> 01:18:03.333
and… and I heard you call.

825
01:18:03.417 --> 01:18:04.708
Oh, yeah.

826
01:18:05.208 --> 01:18:07.000
- Yeah, I...
- Did you…

827
01:18:07.083 --> 01:18:08.583
I was just passing through

828
01:18:08.667 --> 01:18:12.125
and thought I'd just,
you know, check on you.

829
01:18:14.708 --> 01:18:16.833
Doesn't get spooky up this high?

830
01:18:17.375 --> 01:18:19.542
No. I think it's peaceful.

831
01:18:21.042 --> 01:18:24.083
I watch the clouds form
and the light change,

832
01:18:24.167 --> 01:18:26.542
and I get paid to do it.

833
01:18:26.625 --> 01:18:28.208
It's a gift.

834
01:18:29.083 --> 01:18:31.042
Mmm, yeah, I can see that.

835
01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:37.625
How'd you come to this?

836
01:18:38.417 --> 01:18:41.083
I saw a flyer for the job opening

837
01:18:41.167 --> 01:18:42.417
just when I needed it.

838
01:18:43.125 --> 01:18:44.833
Here, fire weed.

839
01:18:44.917 --> 01:18:46.167
Oh, thank you.

840
01:18:47.583 --> 01:18:50.250
I had to get a recommendation
from a family friend.

841
01:18:51.167 --> 01:18:52.542
In his words,

842
01:18:52.625 --> 01:18:54.833
"Claire Thompson is absolutely devoid

843
01:18:54.917 --> 01:18:57.792
of the timidity normally associated
with her sex."

844
01:18:57.875 --> 01:19:00.750
"She possesses
more than adequate work ethic…"

845
01:19:01.917 --> 01:19:05.542
"…and is unafraid of anything
that walks, creeps, or flies."

846
01:19:08.000 --> 01:19:09.708
Hmm.

847
01:19:09.792 --> 01:19:11.000
How is it?

848
01:19:11.083 --> 01:19:12.208
Mmm.

849
01:19:12.292 --> 01:19:13.750
It's good. Thank you.

850
01:19:22.250 --> 01:19:25.500
Everything looks so small from up here.

851
01:19:27.417 --> 01:19:28.458
Yeah.

852
01:19:31.458 --> 01:19:33.083
You want to take a look?

853
01:19:40.542 --> 01:19:42.750
- Everything grows so fast.
- Mm-hmm.

854
01:19:42.833 --> 01:19:45.417
You can barely tell
a fire came through here.

855
01:19:45.500 --> 01:19:47.167
It's like it didn't even happen.

856
01:19:55.708 --> 01:19:57.500
Were you here when it came through?

857
01:20:03.625 --> 01:20:04.625
No.

858
01:20:06.542 --> 01:20:07.542
I wasn't.

859
01:20:10.250 --> 01:20:11.250
But, uh…

860
01:20:12.708 --> 01:20:16.917
My wife and… and my baby, uh…

861
01:20:21.625 --> 01:20:24.042
Yeah. They, uh… They didn't make it out.

862
01:20:25.000 --> 01:20:26.458
Oh, Robert.

863
01:20:26.542 --> 01:20:29.792
Yeah. You see, sometimes it… it…

864
01:20:32.042 --> 01:20:35.458
It feels like the sadness
will just eat me alive, but…

865
01:20:38.125 --> 01:20:42.417
Sometimes it just… It feels like it…
it happened to somebody else.

866
01:20:46.625 --> 01:20:48.333
But no, I wasn't here.

867
01:20:51.375 --> 01:20:52.792
Uh, not when, uh…

868
01:20:54.583 --> 01:20:57.292
Not when…
when I should have been, I guess,

869
01:20:57.375 --> 01:21:01.292
and when… when, uh,
they needed me the most.

870
01:21:09.500 --> 01:21:10.833
I hear them sometimes.

871
01:21:13.458 --> 01:21:14.792
Out there in the woods.

872
01:21:16.792 --> 01:21:19.125
Just talking and laughing.

873
01:21:21.167 --> 01:21:22.167
Yeah.

874
01:21:23.500 --> 01:21:26.792
I don't even want to turn my head
because I'm afraid…

875
01:21:28.542 --> 01:21:31.375
That I'll scare them away.

876
01:21:34.792 --> 01:21:35.875
So I just listen…

877
01:21:39.458 --> 01:21:41.292
Until they fade away somewhere else…

878
01:21:44.500 --> 01:21:45.875
Wherever those things go.

879
01:21:52.833 --> 01:21:55.250
Never told anybody else that.

880
01:21:59.292 --> 01:22:01.167
You think that makes me sound crazy?

881
01:22:09.875 --> 01:22:14.000
Yeah. I lost my husband too,
a little over a year ago.

882
01:22:15.333 --> 01:22:17.333
It took him a long time.

883
01:22:18.875 --> 01:22:21.125
And when it was over,
it was like there was a…

884
01:22:21.792 --> 01:22:23.083
A hole in the world.

885
01:22:24.792 --> 01:22:27.042
I had more questions than answers,

886
01:22:27.125 --> 01:22:30.333
like no human had ever died before.

887
01:22:32.125 --> 01:22:36.708
When you go through something like that,
nothing you do is crazy.

888
01:22:38.292 --> 01:22:40.708
You just go through what you go through.

889
01:22:40.792 --> 01:22:41.792
Mmm.

890
01:22:43.792 --> 01:22:46.708
In the forest,
every least thing's important.

891
01:22:47.375 --> 01:22:49.125
It's all threaded together,

892
01:22:49.208 --> 01:22:51.833
so you can't tell where one thing ends
and another begins

893
01:22:51.917 --> 01:22:53.250
if you really look at it.

894
01:22:56.042 --> 01:22:59.750
The little insects you can't even see,
they play a role as vital as the river.

895
01:23:01.667 --> 01:23:04.500
The dead tree is as important
as the living one.

896
01:23:08.250 --> 01:23:10.875
There must be something
for us to learn from that.

897
01:23:12.917 --> 01:23:14.833
What if you got nothing left to give?

898
01:23:16.958 --> 01:23:18.417
Hmm? What then?

899
01:23:19.167 --> 01:23:20.167
Mmm…

900
01:23:21.750 --> 01:23:25.167
The world needs a hermit in the woods
as much as a preacher in the pulpit.

901
01:23:30.083 --> 01:23:32.417
Yeah. Yeah.

902
01:23:33.792 --> 01:23:34.958
Is that what I am?

903
01:23:36.750 --> 01:23:37.750
A hermit?

904
01:23:38.750 --> 01:23:41.708
Well, I mean,
I believe we both are, in our own ways.

905
01:23:44.833 --> 01:23:47.292
Just waiting to see
what we've been left here for.

906
01:24:58.542 --> 01:25:01.042
<i>Though he knew it must be impossible,</i>

907
01:25:01.125 --> 01:25:04.708
<i>the thought overwhelmed him
that his daughter had returned.</i>

908
01:25:05.667 --> 01:25:06.667
Katie?

909
01:25:11.875 --> 01:25:12.958
Kate.

910
01:25:13.625 --> 01:25:14.625
Katie?

911
01:25:17.167 --> 01:25:18.333
Oh, Kate.

912
01:25:18.958 --> 01:25:20.375
Okay.

913
01:25:34.708 --> 01:25:35.708
Okay.

914
01:25:41.458 --> 01:25:42.708
Okay.

915
01:25:46.208 --> 01:25:47.458
Oh my God.

916
01:25:48.542 --> 01:25:50.042
Honey, your leg's broken.

917
01:25:50.750 --> 01:25:53.292
This is gonna hurt, just for a moment.

918
01:26:00.042 --> 01:26:01.417
Okay.

919
01:26:18.417 --> 01:26:19.500
Katie.

920
01:26:24.500 --> 01:26:25.500
No.

921
01:26:57.250 --> 01:26:58.250
Okay.

922
01:27:24.125 --> 01:27:26.083
You want to get some water, honey?

923
01:27:26.167 --> 01:27:27.458
- Yeah.
- All right.

924
01:27:28.167 --> 01:27:29.167
Up.

925
01:27:33.417 --> 01:27:34.875
Wow.

926
01:27:34.958 --> 01:27:36.250
What did you do?

927
01:27:36.792 --> 01:27:38.125
What did you do that for?

928
01:27:42.208 --> 01:27:44.125
Water!

929
01:27:52.667 --> 01:27:53.667
Bye-bye.

930
01:27:54.542 --> 01:27:55.542
Bye-bye.

931
01:27:56.458 --> 01:27:57.458
Bye-bye.

932
01:28:18.125 --> 01:28:19.125
Katie?

933
01:28:27.083 --> 01:28:28.083
Kate?

934
01:28:34.083 --> 01:28:35.083
Kate!

935
01:28:37.917 --> 01:28:38.917
Kate!

936
01:29:03.875 --> 01:29:07.542
<i>Seeing Kate again felt as real
as anything else in his life,</i>

937
01:29:08.583 --> 01:29:11.333
<i>even as he wondered
if it had really happened to him.</i>

938
01:29:14.875 --> 01:29:19.500
<i>He spent many days and evenings wandering
the forests and fields of the region,</i>

939
01:29:20.167 --> 01:29:21.917
<i>looking for any sign of her.</i>

940
01:29:28.042 --> 01:29:29.375
<i>Though he found none,</i>

941
01:29:30.208 --> 01:29:32.208
<i>he spent the rest of his days waiting</i>

942
01:29:33.042 --> 01:29:36.250
<i>so he would be there
if she ever came back.</i>

943
01:30:33.208 --> 01:30:34.833
<i>In his later years,</i>

944
01:30:34.917 --> 01:30:38.667
<i>Grainier rode the Great Northern
from time to time into Spokane.</i>

945
01:30:43.208 --> 01:30:46.542
<i>He wandered the city
without much direction or purpose,</i>

946
01:30:46.625 --> 01:30:50.625
<i>as if looking for something
he had misplaced a long time ago.</i>

947
01:30:55.333 --> 01:30:57.667
<i>Roger, zero G, and I feel fine.</i>

948
01:30:57.750 --> 01:30:59.667
<i>Capsule is turning around.</i>

949
01:31:00.542 --> 01:31:02.583
<i>Oh, that view is tremendous.</i>

950
01:31:03.542 --> 01:31:05.125
What is that fellow doing?

951
01:31:05.208 --> 01:31:06.333
<i>Roger, capsule…</i>

952
01:31:06.417 --> 01:31:07.708
He's in outer space.

953
01:31:07.792 --> 01:31:11.417
<i>I could see the booster during turnaround
just a couple of hundred yards behind me.</i>

954
01:31:11.500 --> 01:31:12.500
Huh.

955
01:31:13.125 --> 01:31:14.500
<i>Roger…</i>

956
01:31:14.583 --> 01:31:15.792
So is that…?

957
01:31:16.375 --> 01:31:17.458
That's us.

958
01:31:18.042 --> 01:31:21.375
<i>Roger. Understand go
for at least seven orbits.</i>

959
01:31:22.833 --> 01:31:24.000
Huh.

960
01:31:24.083 --> 01:31:26.458
<i>…a camel spit gold from his teeth.</i>

961
01:31:26.542 --> 01:31:27.708
Gold!

962
01:31:27.792 --> 01:31:32.042
Witness Sun Tzu,
the holy man from the far, far east.

963
01:31:32.125 --> 01:31:35.208
Sun Tzu, the holy man,
will read your dreams.

964
01:31:35.292 --> 01:31:38.208
Experience the world-famous
Bittler Sisters,

965
01:31:38.292 --> 01:31:41.958
or better yet,
pay you a dime to see a monster.

966
01:31:42.042 --> 01:31:43.792
You. You.

967
01:31:43.875 --> 01:31:47.083
Come here. Come here.
Yeah. Cast the stone aside.

968
01:31:47.167 --> 01:31:48.417
Inside this theater,

969
01:31:48.500 --> 01:31:52.708
the mysteries of the world flit about
like bats and insects.

970
01:31:52.792 --> 01:31:56.375
Here, all the answers to everything.
You buy a ticket…

971
01:31:56.458 --> 01:31:59.667
<i>That night in the theater,
he took in a curiosity show</i>

972
01:31:59.750 --> 01:32:01.875
<i>that promised a vision of a monster</i>

973
01:32:02.792 --> 01:32:04.750
<i>but was only a boy in a costume.</i>

974
01:32:09.458 --> 01:32:13.625
<i>He saw his face in a mirror
for the first time in nearly a decade.</i>

975
01:32:15.125 --> 01:32:18.500
<i>He could see now the toll
that the years had taken.</i>

976
01:32:20.750 --> 01:32:22.833
<i>He felt that he was only just beginning</i>

977
01:32:22.917 --> 01:32:26.292
<i>to have some faint understanding
of his life,</i>

978
01:32:26.375 --> 01:32:28.833
<i>even though it was now slipping away
from him.</i>

979
01:32:33.208 --> 01:32:37.583
Just four dollars,
see the world as only the birds do.

980
01:33:12.042 --> 01:33:14.292
Hey, you'd better hold on to something.

981
01:33:15.583 --> 01:33:16.625
Huh?

982
01:33:31.167 --> 01:33:32.792
<i>Robert.</i>

983
01:34:14.333 --> 01:34:15.667
Beautiful, ain't it?

984
01:34:17.917 --> 01:34:18.958
<i>Just beautiful.</i>

985
01:34:22.542 --> 01:34:24.625
<i>When Robert Grainier died in his sleep</i>

986
01:34:24.708 --> 01:34:28.000
<i>sometime in November of 1968,</i>

987
01:34:28.083 --> 01:34:31.000
<i>his life ended as quietly as it had begun.</i>

988
01:34:32.042 --> 01:34:35.958
<i>He'd never purchased a firearm
or spoken into a telephone.</i>

989
01:34:37.250 --> 01:34:39.917
<i>He had no idea
who his parents might have been,</i>

990
01:34:40.000 --> 01:34:42.000
<i>and he left no heirs behind him.</i>

991
01:34:44.625 --> 01:34:46.167
<i>But on that spring day,</i>

992
01:34:46.958 --> 01:34:50.417
<i>as he misplaced all sense of up and down,</i>

993
01:34:51.250 --> 01:34:55.750
<i>he felt, at last, connected to it all.</i>

994
01:35:44.375 --> 01:35:47.000
<i>I seen a grizzly big as a house</i>

995
01:35:47.667 --> 01:35:50.292
<i>Walk across an open plain</i>

996
01:35:51.125 --> 01:35:54.125
<i>Heard of a boy called Elvis something</i>

997
01:35:54.208 --> 01:35:57.458
<i>His voice could drive
Young girls insane</i>

998
01:35:58.000 --> 01:36:00.667
<i>I've seen a man from a mile away</i>

999
01:36:01.333 --> 01:36:04.417
<i>Shoot a bobcat through the brain but</i>

1000
01:36:04.500 --> 01:36:07.875
<i>Lately I've been having dreams</i>

1001
01:36:07.958 --> 01:36:11.167
<i>Crazy dreams I can't explain</i>

1002
01:36:11.792 --> 01:36:14.708
<i>A woman standing
In a field of flowers and</i>

1003
01:36:15.250 --> 01:36:17.792
<i>A screaming locomotive train</i>

1004
01:36:18.542 --> 01:36:21.500
<i>Crazy dreams that go on for hours</i>

1005
01:36:22.125 --> 01:36:25.875
<i>And I can't begin
To tell you how that feels</i>

1006
01:36:39.250 --> 01:36:42.125
<i>I've seen an elk with twisted antlers</i>

1007
01:36:42.208 --> 01:36:45.000
<i>Throw bright lightning across the sky</i>

1008
01:36:45.750 --> 01:36:48.417
<i>Seen a man with a broken curse</i>

1009
01:36:49.125 --> 01:36:52.250
<i>Leap from a bridge and try to fly</i>

1010
01:36:52.833 --> 01:36:55.417
<i>Seen a boy who was a dog</i>

1011
01:36:56.000 --> 01:36:58.875
<i>Who became a man who forgot to die</i>

1012
01:36:59.667 --> 01:37:02.292
<i>Lately I've been having dreams</i>

1013
01:37:03.000 --> 01:37:06.417
<i>Crazy dreams I can't explain</i>

1014
01:37:06.500 --> 01:37:09.542
<i>A woman standing
In a field of flowers and</i>

1015
01:37:10.042 --> 01:37:12.708
<i>A screaming locomotive train</i>

1016
01:37:13.458 --> 01:37:16.375
<i>Crazy dreams that go on for hours</i>

1017
01:37:16.958 --> 01:37:21.250
<i>And I can't begin
To tell you how that feels</i>

1018
01:37:47.625 --> 01:37:51.125
<i>The space that connects me
Where I am now</i>

1019
01:37:51.208 --> 01:37:54.042
<i>To the place where I'll one day be</i>

1020
01:37:54.542 --> 01:37:57.250
<i>It's measured in the words
That we speak</i>

1021
01:37:57.333 --> 01:38:00.208
<i>And the strange and wondrous things
I've seen</i>

1022
01:38:01.083 --> 01:38:04.458
<i>It's measured in truth,
It's measured in love</i>

1023
01:38:04.542 --> 01:38:07.375
<i>Measured in a tendency to pain</i>

1024
01:38:07.958 --> 01:38:11.667
<i>Measured by a girl
In a field of flowers</i>

1025
01:38:11.750 --> 01:38:14.708
<i>A screaming dream of a midnight train</i>

1026
01:38:15.375 --> 01:38:17.708
<i>This has been going on for years</i>

1027
01:38:18.208 --> 01:38:21.583
<i>Years and years and years and years</i>

1028
01:38:21.667 --> 01:38:25.000
<i>Years and years and years and years</i>

1029
01:38:25.083 --> 01:38:28.542
<i>I can't begin to tell you
How that feels</i>

1030
01:40:29.250 --> 01:40:32.542
<i>If the Lord was a redwood</i>

1031
01:40:33.208 --> 01:40:35.708
<i>Would you try to cut Him down?</i>

1032
01:40:36.708 --> 01:40:41.833
<i>Or climb up His loving branches
And look around?</i>

1033
01:40:43.333 --> 01:40:46.333
<i>If the river was the tears</i>

1034
01:40:46.875 --> 01:40:49.708
<i>Of all those who've passed</i>

1035
01:40:50.417 --> 01:40:53.042
<i>Would you try to dam it up</i>

1036
01:40:54.167 --> 01:40:55.625
<i>Or let it pass?</i>
